  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft continues as the CWA resides on the \nnortheastern edge of the western upper ridge, with longwave\ntroughing to the east northeast across New England and south of\na shortwave trough moving across southern Manitoba into the\nUpper Midwest. At the surface, a 1020 mb high is centered over\nthe Chicagoland area, yielding light easterly winds across the \nCWA. Pleasant conditions abound across the area once again this \nmorning, with temperatures as of 3 AM ranging form the mid 60s \nto upper 60s. After a couple days of cooler temperatures, \nafternoon highs today should rebound back into the upper 80s to \neven the lower 90s (mid 80s across NE Missouri), with moisture \nalso recovering as dewpoints should increase considerably \nthroughout the day today. By later this afternoon, strengthening\nnorthwesterly flow is expected across the Central Plains, while\na front progresses southeastward across the Central High \nPlains, intercepting a moist and moderately unstable boundary \nlater. This should help initiate convection across southwestern \nNebraska by around 4 pm today, with this convection likely \ngrowing upscale into clusters or an MCS capable of producing \nsevere winds as it moves to the southeast through the evening \nhours. However, CAMs have trended further west with the \nconvective activity, and it now seems rather unlikely that \nsevere storms could reach our CWA tonight. However, SPC for now \nhas maintained a marginal risk across NW Kansas into far NW \nMissouri, and we will continue to monitor CAM trends. Convection\nmay reignite overnight Tuesday into early Wednesday morning \nacross central and east central Kansas, slowly drifting to the \neast southeast but likely dissipating before entering into our \nCWA. The aforementioned cold front that should help ignite \nstorms today across the Central High Plains should move north to\nsouth through the region on Wednesday afternoon as the \nshortwave trough across the Upper Midwest makes its way eastward\ntoward the Great Lakes. Convection may fire along the front by \nmid afternoon toward the Ozarks Plateau, and SPC has placed \nButler to Moberly and points east into a marginal risk for \nsevere storms. \n\nTemperatures should be a bit cooler on Thursday afternoon \nbehind the front, with highs in the mid 80s for most locations.\nAlso, drier air will filter in behind the front, with dew points\nlikely dropping into the lower 60s by mid Thursday afternoon.\nTemperatures may increase a couple degrees on Friday, but\nhumidity should remain relatively comfortable. By this weekend, \nthe upper ridge axis is progged to drift further east as an \nupper trough comes ashore across the Pacific Northwest (and will\nalso flatten out the ridge). This should lead to increased mid \nlevel heights and warmer 850 temps, translating to surface high \ntemperatures into the lower to mid 90s for Saturday and into the\nmid to perhaps even the upper 90s for Sunday. Moisture return\nwill also increase dew points, and accordingly, heat indices,\nwith heat index values approaching or even exceeding 100 F by\nSunday afternoon.",
